 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Coarse-to-Fine Densification
-A specific variant of the hybrid strategy applied to mesh-based representations. It initializes a sparse mesh via edge-aware sampling and progressively refines it by inserting centroid vertices for high-scoring triangles and re-running Delaunay triangulation [S2]. It is chosen when a continuous surface representation is preferred over discrete points. It sets itself apart by using DSSIM and area-weighted scoring to guide mesh topology changes [S2].
+A specific implementation of the hybrid strategy used in mesh-based neural reconstruction [S2]. It differs from point-based variants by explicitly maintaining a triangulated surface structure. The method initializes a sparse mesh via edge-aware sampling and progressively refines it by inserting centroid vertices for high-scoring triangles and re-running Delaunay triangulation [S2]. It is chosen when a watertight or structured surface representation is desired, and it handles high-quality primitive-based neural reconstruction [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Performance:** The hybrid strategy yields significantly better rendering quality than single-stage training. On the Blender dataset, removing the hybrid strategy reduces PSNR from 30.3 dB to 26.6 dB, SSIM from 0.945 to 0.925, and increases LPIPS from 0.078 to 0.163 [S1].
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training generally takes between 1-3 minutes on an RTX A6000 GPU for the mesh variant, leveraging custom CUDA kernels for rasterization and interpolation [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Improves view synthesis quality by concentrating primitives where they are needed most. Prevents over-smoothing in complex regions and under-representation in simple regions.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of densification intervals and thresholds. Adds computational overhead for scoring and re-meshing (in mesh variants).
+*   **Performance:** On the Blender dataset, removing the hybrid strategy ('w/o hybrid') reduces PSNR from 30.3 dB to 26.6 dB, SSIM from 0.945 to 0.925, and increases LPIPS from 0.078 to 0.163 [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Training generally takes between 1-3 minutes on an RTX A6000 for the full pipeline, including CUDA rasterization [S2]. The densification step adds overhead proportional to the number of triangles and the cost of Delaunay triangulation.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage scales with the number of points/vertices. The hybrid strategy typically results in a higher final vertex count than single-stage training due to densification, but achieves better quality per vertex.
+*   **Strengths:** Significantly better rendering quality than single-stage training [S1]. Stabilizes optimization by separating global structure learning from local detail refinement.
+*   **Limitations:** More complex to implement due to the need for densification logic and re-triangulation. Sensitive to initialization quality.
 
 ## Background
-The Hybrid Coarse-to-Fine Strategy originates from research into differentiable point-based radiance fields, which offer an alternative to volumetric neural rendering by using points without ray-marching [S1]. It was introduced to address the limitations of single-stage training, which often results in suboptimal point distributions. The mesh-based variant (Coarse-to-Fine Densification) was developed in the context of Neural Harmonic Textures for high-quality primitive-based neural reconstruction [S2].
+The Hybrid Coarse-to-Fine Strategy originates from research into differentiable point-based radiance fields for efficient view synthesis [S1]. It was introduced as an alternative to volumetric representations and coordinate-network representations that require ray-marching with hundreds of network evaluations per ray [S1]. The strategy was validated through ablation experiments showing that all components, including the coarse-to-fine approach, contribute to rendering quality [S1]. A related mesh-based variant was proposed for high-quality primitive-based neural reconstruction [S2].
 
